In addition, it was observed that the sensitivity of wasp venom sIgE ranges from 83% up to 97% and is lower than for honeybee venom sIgE—98– 100% [37]. Commercially available Hymenoptera allergens for CRD are:

- • rApi m 1, rApi m 2, rApi m 3, rApi m 5, rApi m 10;
- • rVes v 1, rVes v 5;
- • rPol d 5. The much higher sensitivity of in vitro tests is obtained by performing a componentresolved diagnosis (CRD) [38–40]. This innovative method is an increasingly better diagnostic tool in allergology today. Thanks to CRD, sIgE against single allergenic proteins can be determined.

In double sensitization, the CRD method is often helpful, but its possibilities are still limited and do not yield a resolve for all ambiguous clinical cases [46]. Commercially available Hymenoptera allergens for CRD are:

- • rApi m 1, rApi m 2, rApi m 3, rApi m 5, rApi m 10;
- • rVes v 1, rVes v 5;
- • rPol d 5. In the case of allergy to different wasp species (often between Vespula and Polistes), differential diagnosis is more complicated due to the independence from cross-reactions associated with CCDs [47,48]. This problem certainly requires further research.
